# Observability Pattern — {{ model.names.namespace }}

## Instrumentation

### Tracing

```csharp
private static readonly ActivitySource ActivitySource = new("{{ model.names.namespace }}");

// Start a span for every domain operation:
using var activity = ActivitySource.StartActivity("{{ model.manifest.service_name }}.get-record");
activity?.SetTag("record.id", id);
activity?.SetTag("db.operation", "SELECT");

try
{
    var result = await _repository.GetAsync(id, ct);
    activity?.SetStatus(ActivityStatusCode.Ok);
    return result;
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    // Always mark spans as error on failure — otherwise all spans appear green in the UI
    activity?.SetStatus(ActivityStatusCode.Error, ex.Message);
    activity?.RecordException(ex);
    throw;
}
```

### Baggage Propagation

<!-- [5.1.2] For distributed tracing across services, W3C Baggage must be explicitly forwarded -->

Baggage is automatically propagated by ASP.NET Core for inbound gRPC calls when `AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation()` is configured. For **outbound** HTTP calls via `IHttpClientFactory`, it is also automatic via `AddHttpClientInstrumentation()`.

For **manual propagation** (e.g., when publishing to a message queue):

```csharp
// Write baggage on the outbound call side:
Baggage.SetBaggage("tenant-id", tenantId);
Baggage.SetBaggage("correlation-id", correlationId);

// The baggage values are then available in downstream services:
var tenantId = Baggage.GetBaggage("tenant-id");
```

**Standard baggage keys for Pervaxis services:**

| Key | Description |
|---|---|
| `tenant-id` | Tenant identifier for multi-tenant services |
| `correlation-id` | Business-level correlation ID (not the same as trace ID) |
| `service-version` | Caller's deployed version (for cross-version debugging) |

### Metrics

```csharp
private static readonly Meter Meter = new("{{ model.names.namespace }}");
private static readonly Counter<long> RpcCount =
    Meter.CreateCounter<long>("{{ model.manifest.service_name }}.rpc.count",
        description: "Number of RPC calls by method and result");
private static readonly Histogram<double> RpcDuration =
    Meter.CreateHistogram<double>("{{ model.manifest.service_name }}.rpc.duration.ms",
        unit: "ms", description: "RPC handler duration in milliseconds");

// Record on every call (from ServiceImpl):
var sw = Stopwatch.GetTimestamp();
try
{
    var result = await continuation(request, context);
    RpcCount.Add(1, new TagList { { "method", context.Method }, { "result", "success" } });
    return result;
}
catch (Exception)
{
    RpcCount.Add(1, new TagList { { "method", context.Method }, { "result", "error" } });
    throw;
}
finally
{
    RpcDuration.Record(Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(sw).TotalMilliseconds,
        new TagList { { "method", context.Method } });
}
```

---

## SLOs and Alert Thresholds

<!-- [5.3.1] Metric names, baselines, and suggested alert thresholds -->

| Metric | Description | Suggested alert |
|---|---|---|
| `{{ model.manifest.service_name }}.rpc.duration.ms` (p99) | 99th-percentile RPC latency | Alert if p99 > 500 ms for 5 min |
| `{{ model.manifest.service_name }}.rpc.duration.ms` (p999) | Worst-case RPC latency | Alert if p999 > 2000 ms for 5 min |
| `{{ model.manifest.service_name }}.rpc.count{result=error}` rate | Error rate | Alert if error rate > 1% over 5 min |
| `aspnetcore.routing.match_attempts{outcome=failure}` | Unrouted requests | Alert if > 0 sustained (misconfiguration signal) |
| `http.server.request.duration` (p95, /healthz) | Health probe latency | Alert if p95 > 100 ms |
| `process.runtime.dotnet.gc.collections` | GC pressure | Alert if Gen2 > 5/min sustained |

**Error budget:** Define your error budget as `(1 - target_availability) * rolling_window_requests`. Example: 99.9% availability on a 1M req/day service = 1000 tolerated errors/day.

**Runbook links:** Add links to your team's runbook for each alert.

---

## Logging Rules

1. **Never log PII.** Log IDs and operation names only. Use `[REDACTED]` or the `ForgeDataClassification` redaction service.
2. Use **structured properties** — not string interpolation — in `LogXxx` calls.
3. Log **request IDs** and **trace IDs** on every log line (the audit middleware does this automatically).
4. Use `LogWarning` for recoverable errors (not found, validation fail). Use `LogError` for unexpected exceptions.
5. Never log at `Debug` in production hot paths — use `LogInformation` or `LogWarning` only.
